WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.860
Narrator: The third
season of "True Detective"

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:09.300
follows a disturbing crime as it unfolds

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.830
over three different time periods

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:16.040
from 1980 to 1990 to 2015.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.400
The present-day period shows
Mahershala Ali's character,

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.030
detective Wayne Hays, as a 70-year-old man

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:24.840
who's still haunted by the case

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.573
while grappling with his own memory loss.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.540
Ali is wholly convincing in old age

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.390
thanks to the power of his performance

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.360
and the makeup artistry of Emmy-nominated

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.590
prosthetic artist Mike Marino.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:40.100
Jimmy Kimmel: And your old-man makeup.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.450
Mahershala: Mike Marino, Mike Marino, man.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.066
Jimmy: Is the best old-man
makeup I've ever seen

00:00:45.066 --> 00:00:46.533
on any actor.

00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:49.020
Narrator: We visited Mike
at his studio in New Jersey

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.720
to find out how he turned
Ali into an old man

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:52.953
for "True Detective."

00:00:59.662 --> 00:01:02.470
Mike is the makeup designer
behind "Black Swan,"

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:05.460
"Wolf of Wall Street," "Birdman,"

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.440
and Heidi Klum's elaborate
Halloween costumes.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.910
On "True Detective," he
was in charge of the makeup

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:15.450
and wigs seen on Mahershala
Ali and Stephen Dorff,

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.680
who plays Wayne's partner
Roland in the show.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.370
Mike has a ton of
experience adding decades

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:22.870
to actors' faces.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:25.820
But his transformative
work was really spotlighted

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.040
in "True Detective"

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.870
because of the unconventional way

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:31.490
that the show's narrative was structured.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:35.130
Mike: They're intercutting
in between time periods,

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.820
so you really get a chance
to see the difference

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.810
and the contrast between Mahershala's face

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.520
and Stephen Dorff's face.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.540
Narrator: The first step
in the transformation

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:47.120
was deciding where exactly
the prosthetics would go.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.820
Mike: When you're designing a makeup,

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:52.290
you have to find all these
lines and creases naturally

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.460
that that actor may have,

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.120
so that when you glue pieces onto it,

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.880
it doesn't move strange.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.270
Mahershala's skin is so perfect
that there's no landmarks

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.110
to hide a wrinkle in

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.080
or hide a prosthetic inside,

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.580
so it's really challenging
finding the place

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.390
where prosthetics can live.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.870
Narrator: Next, to actually
make the prosthetics,

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.620
you have to take a life
cast of the actor's face,

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.353
basically creating a
three-dimensional copy of it.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.840
Mike: We start out with a
life cast of Mahershala.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.440
I take that life cast, and
I cast it out in plaster,

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.150
and I take little pieces of clay,

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:40.780
and I add a little where I feel

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.450
is going to look the best

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.390
as far as wrinkles and puffy dents,

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.310
and I add in wrinkles, and
I sculpt everything down

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.100
to a very fine detail.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:51.140
Narrator: The sculptures run under water,

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.070
then the pieces of clay are removed

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.970
and put on individual molds

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:57.520
from which the prosthetic pieces are made.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.830
Mike: On set, I take those
pieces and glue onto Mahershala

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:01.663
and Stephen Dorff

00:03:01.663 --> 00:03:04.840
all these prosthetics
that were once sculptures.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.720
Once that is done, they're airbrushed

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.690
to look like real skin,
freckles, little imperfections,

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:11.523
things like that.

00:03:11.523 --> 00:03:13.000
Once it's all painted,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.570
we glue wigs on, make sure
their sheen and reflection

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:18.370
of the skin is really perfect.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.620
It really took about three hours

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.990
to glue on the prosthetics each day.

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:25.180
Narrator: All that had to last

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.163
throughout a 12- to 16-hour shooting day.

00:03:32.665 --> 00:03:34.450
Mike: It's a battle each day.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:37.240
I'm maintaining the makeup as well.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.850
You know, in "True Detective"
we filmed in Arkansas

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:43.750
in the summer, so it was really hot.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:47.600
So it was also an engineering feat

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.690
to really kind of prep
their skin to not sweat,

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.510
put them in air conditioning tents,

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:56.270
put Mahershala in a dark room

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.940
where he's not in sunlight
and he's sweating.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.010
Narrator: Mike's work on the show

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.570
wasn't limited to the actors' faces.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:05.020
His team also built a life-size replica

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.470
for Will Purcell,

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:09.110
the missing boy whose
body Wayne finds in a cave

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:10.763
at the end of the first episode.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:15.730
But the aging transformations
were his real masterpiece.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.010
Mike: You can see how they'd
most likely age in real life

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.690
'cause I try to find on their own face,

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:25.480
where if I find any little
wrinkle or line or crease,

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:26.800
I accentuate that.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:29.680
I'm kind of a reverse makeup artist.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:33.710
A makeup artist is taking
out all the flaws and things

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.820
on someone's face, and I'm adding them in.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.143
So I'm really an actor's worst nightmare.

